  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\,({tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta +1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\,{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify and use $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta {sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta =({sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta .\n$$",
